{"context":"- The delivery lifecycle specification is the authority layer of the three-layer model (§5.1): it defines what a legal lifecycle *is*; the Anvil Runtime (Core) implements and enforces it; delivery lifecycle standards supply framework content.\n- In v1.x, one project version (v1.4.x → v1.5.0) versions everything. Contracts live as Go types in `internal/contracts`, so a contract change ships inside an engine release — engine releases and contract changes are conflated.\n- The Manifesto (§3.6) states that a change to the contract is a Core change with ecosystem-wide impact and must be treated as such.\n- The transition plan (A2) requires: the specification carries its own version line; Core declares which contract versions it implements; standards declare which contract version they target.\n- Th Source: ADR-024-contract-specification-versioning-and-compatibility-policy.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"1. The delivery lifecycle specification carries its own independent semver version line (major.minor.patch), decoupled from Anvil Runtime (Core) releases. The contract major version is the unit of compatibility.\n2. Core declares which contract major versions it implements; delivery lifecycle standards declare which contract version they target (Manifest and Compatibility parts, §5.4).\n3. A breaking (major) contract change is a governed event: it requires an ADR and ships with a Core major version (Manifesto §3.6; §12.1: the first contract major bump is post-v2.0).\n4. Concrete bounds: the Anvil Runtime implements at most TWO concurrently supported contract major versions. A superseded contract major remains supported for one full contract generation — until the next contract major ships and","consequences":"- Core maintenance carries the cost of implementing at most two contract major versions.\n- Standards must declare their target contract version; the registry rejects non-declared or incompatible ones (ADR-023).\n- The first contract major bump is a governed event after v2.0 (§12.1) and may invalidate standards that have not migrated to a supported contract major.\n- Version tooling must validate compatibility automatically (mitigates the §11.1 release-stall risk).\n- The conformance harness (EPIC-013) validates engine behavior against the declared contract versions.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Keep the single version line (status quo).** Rejected: conflation with engine releases blocks independent standard evolution and hides breaking changes inside engine releases.\n- **Version the specification but support all contract majors without bound.** Rejected: unbounded implementation and test cost in the Anvil Runtime; the plan requires bounds (§4.6, §8).\n- **Move the specification to a separate repository to gain versioning independence.** Rejected for v2: the specification"}